WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.115
REST API security includes predictable
resource behavior.

2
00:00:03.195 --> 00:00:06.117
A service must handle requests that are
slow, malformed, repeated,

3
00:00:06.197 --> 00:00:08.145
expensive, cancelled, or partially failed.

4
00:00:08.225 --> 00:00:11.763
Go gives developers useful tools through
context, explicit handlers, timeouts,

5
00:00:11.843 --> 00:00:14.868
and concurrency primitives, but those
tools need deliberate design.

6
00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:15.973
Start by bounding work.

7
00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:18.411
Request body limits, header limits where
applicable,

8
00:00:18.491 --> 00:00:20.799
JSON decoding limits, pagination, file
size limits,

9
00:00:20.879 --> 00:00:24.262
and careful parsing prevent a single
request from consuming more memory, CPU,

10
00:00:24.342 --> 00:00:25.829
or time than the feature requires.

11
00:00:25.909 --> 00:00:28.268
Limits should match the business purpose
of the route,

12
00:00:28.348 --> 00:00:30.398
not just a default copied from another
service.

13
00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:33.606
Timeouts and context cancellation should
travel through the call path.

14
00:00:33.686 --> 00:00:36.454
HTTP servers, HTTP clients, database
operations, queue calls,

15
00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:38.893
and downstream services should have clear
deadlines.

16
00:00:38.973 --> 00:00:40.972
If a client disconnects or a request times
out,

17
00:00:41.052 --> 00:00:44.385
related work should stop unless there is a
specific reason to continue under a

18
00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:45.182
separate owner.

19
00:00:45.262 --> 00:00:46.954
Goroutines need lifecycle ownership.

20
00:00:47.034 --> 00:00:50.213
A handler should not start background work
that has no cancellation path,

21
00:00:50.293 --> 00:00:51.780
no error reporting, or no cleanup.

22
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.269
Files, response bodies, database rows,
locks, timers,

23
00:00:54.349 --> 00:00:56.862
and network connections should be released
predictably.

24
00:00:56.942 --> 00:00:58.890
Rate limits, bounded queues, backpressure,

25
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.841
and safe degradation help the service stay
useful under pressure.

26
00:01:01.921 --> 00:01:03.561
The goal is not to guess every failure.

27
00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:05.897
It is to make resource use intentional,
observable,

28
00:01:05.977 --> 00:01:09.105
and recoverable so the API behaves safely
when conditions are not ideal.

29
00:01:09.185 --> 00:01:11.953
Resource controls should be reviewed as
part of feature design,

30
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.571
not only after production trouble.

31
00:01:13.651 --> 00:01:16.420
A route that exports data, imports files,
sends notifications,

32
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.576
or calls a slow dependency needs different
bounds than a simple lookup.

33
00:01:19.656 --> 00:01:22.117
Clear limits make those differences
visible, testable,

34
00:01:22.197 --> 00:01:23.940
and easier to operate during incidents.
